  "notes": "The compositional half of the same Toronto alt-R&B axis, encoded on the alt-R&B mode the Lane line names first, so `halfTime` is true at `halftime_3` with the retro-synth mode's 118—132 inside the band. Where his co-producer's entry is about reverb, this one is about the switch between two whole percussion sets: \"sparse half-time kick/snare with reverb-heavy claps for the R&B mode; a gated 1980s snare on 2&4 with a four-square kick for the retro-synth mode. Percussion changes wholesale between sections.\" That wholesale change is a `percs.densityPerBar` that runs 0—5 and a `switchUpProb` of 0.2, and the gated retro snare is the `large` reverb with a `hard` transient — the opposite pairing to the soft-in-a-hall snare of the other half of the axis. \"He DJ'd from 13 and produces in FL Studio, so patterns are pattern-block based rather than linear\" is the high `quantizeStrength` under a moderate velocity spread. The 808 is authored because the entry names it and makes it the mode marker: \"sustained 808/sub in the R&B mode; a pumping analogue-style synth bass on 8ths in the retro mode — the low end is what signals which mode a track is in\", so `bass808` is `counter_riff` with a legato sustain while the bassline runs `offbeat_8ths` for the retro half. Harmony is \"minor keys with a strong ii—V or VI—VII cadence and a taste for 1980s synth-pop harmony\", carried as those two families, and \"loops are 4 or 8 bars with real harmonic motion\" is why `vamp` is absent from `harmonicRhythm`. \"Countermelody is a synth arpeggio\" is `arp` at top weight. ⚠ `confidence` is medium and the research says why: \"harmonic/drum detail inferred from the records and marked\". Every drum probability, the humanize numbers and `bpm.mode` 70 (a felt-spine reading of the same 60—150 band his co-producer's entry folds) are INFERRED.",
